        "question": {
          ":question/id": "cfa90d45-1c35-5375-90b4-56db8a1d7b00",
          ":question/problem": "Which of the following equations has infinitely many solutions?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $-3x+7=-3x+7$\n\n- **II.** $4x=5x+7$\n\n- **III.** $-2x+9=-2x+9$",
          ":question/worked-solution": "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3x+7 & =-3x+7 \\\\ -3x+7+3x & =-3x+7+3x \\\\ 7 & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $-3x+7=-3x+7$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x & =5x+7 \\\\ 4x-4x & =5x+7-4x \\\\ 0 & =x+7 \\\\ 0-7 & =x+7-7 \\\\ x & =-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $4x=5x+7$ has the unique solution $x=-7.$\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x+9 & =-2x+9 \\\\ -2x+9+2x & =-2x+9+2x \\\\ 9 & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $-2x+9=-2x+9$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
          ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
