          ":question/id": "ac4df106-7b13-5306-b058-8470d473985b",
          ":question/problem": "Which of the following equations has infinitely many solutions?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $-3x-6=-3x-6$\n\n- **II.** $4x+6=5x+3$\n\n- **III.** $6x-3=6x-3$",
          ":question/worked-solution": "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3x-6 & =-3x-6 \\\\ -3x-6+3x & =-3x-6+3x \\\\ -6 & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $-3x-6=-3x-6$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+6 & =5x+3 \\\\ 4x+6-4x & =5x+3-4x \\\\ 6 & =x+3 \\\\ 6-3 & =x+3-3 \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $4x+6=5x+3$ has the unique solution $x=3.$\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x-3 & =6x-3 \\\\ 6x-3-6x & =6x-3-6x \\\\ -3 & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $6x-3=6x-3$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
