          ":question/id": "29e3990b-84bd-5fcf-affc-18cc858b4d8e",
          ":question/problem": "Which of the following equations has infinitely many solutions?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $-4\\left(3x-2\\right)-2x+2=-4\\left(3x-3\\right)-2x-2$\n\n- **II.** $-2\\left(2x+1\\right)-3x+4=-2\\left(-3x-4\\right)+x-4$\n\n- **III.** $-3\\left(2x-3\\right)+x-3=2\\left(-3x+2\\right)+x+2$",
          ":question/worked-solution": "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4\\left(3x-2\\right)-2x+2 & =-4\\left(3x-3\\right)-2x-2 \\\\ \\left(-4\\right)\\cdot\\left(3x\\right)+\\left(-4\\right)\\cdot\\left(-2\\right)-2x+2 & =\\left(-4\\right)\\cdot\\left(3x\\right)+\\left(-4\\right)\\cdot\\left(-3\\right)-2x-2 \\\\ -12x+8-2x+2 & =-12x+12-2x-2 \\\\ -14x+10 & =-14x+10 \\\\ -14x+10+14x & =-14x+10+14x \\\\ 10 & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $-4\\left(3x-2\\right)-2x+2=-4\\left(3x-3\\right)-2x-2$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2\\left(2x+1\\right)-3x+4 & =-2\\left(-3x-4\\right)+x-4 \\\\ \\left(-2\\right)\\cdot\\left(2x\\right)+\\left(-2\\right)\\cdot\\left(1\\right)-3x+4 & =\\left(-2\\right)\\cdot\\left(-3x\\right)+\\left(-2\\right)\\cdot\\left(-4\\right)+x-4 \\\\ -4x-2-3x+4 & =6x+8+x-4 \\\\ -7x+2 & =7x+4 \\\\ -7x+2+7x & =7x+4+7x \\\\ 2 & =14x+4 \\\\ 2-4 & =14x+4-4 \\\\ -2 & =14x \\\\ x & =- \\frac{1}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $-2\\left(2x+1\\right)-3x+4=-2\\left(-3x-4\\right)+x-4$ has the unique solution $x=- \\frac{1}{7}.$\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3\\left(2x-3\\right)+x-3 & =2\\left(-3x+2\\right)+x+2 \\\\ \\left(-3\\right)\\cdot\\left(2x\\right)+\\left(-3\\right)\\cdot\\left(-3\\right)+x-3 & =\\left(2\\right)\\cdot\\left(-3x\\right)+\\left(2\\right)\\cdot\\left(2\\right)+x+2 \\\\ -6x+9+x-3 & =-6x+4+x+2 \\\\ -5x+6 & =-5x+6 \\\\ -5x+6+5x & =-5x+6+5x \\\\ 6 & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $-3\\left(2x-3\\right)+x-3=2\\left(-3x+2\\right)+x+2$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
