        "question": {
          ":question/id": "2030ef91-b525-53c6-a517-824465bbb34b",
          ":question/problem": "Which of the following equations has infinitely many solutions?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $2\\left(x+4\\right)=2x+8$\n\n- **II.** $5\\left(x+2\\right)=-2x+6$\n\n- **III.** $3\\left(x+5\\right)=3x+15$",
          ":question/worked-solution": "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\left(x+4\\right) & =2x+8 \\\\ 2\\cdot x+2\\cdot 4 & =2x+8 \\\\ 2x+8 & =2x+8 \\\\ 2x+8-2x & =2x+8-2x \\\\ 8 & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $2\\left(x+4\\right)=2x+8$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\left(x+2\\right) & =-2x+6 \\\\ 5\\cdot x+5\\cdot 2 & =-2x+6 \\\\ 5x+10 & =-2x+6 \\\\ 5x+10+2x & =-2x+6+2x \\\\ 7x+10 & =6 \\\\ 7x+10-10 & =6-10 \\\\ 7x & =-4 \\\\ x & =- \\frac{4}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $5\\left(x+2\\right)=-2x+6$ has the unique solution $x=- \\frac{4}{7}.$\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\left(x+5\\right) & =3x+15 \\\\ 3\\cdot x+3\\cdot 5 & =3x+15 \\\\ 3x+15 & =3x+15 \\\\ 3x+15-3x & =3x+15-3x \\\\ 15 & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $3\\left(x+5\\right)=3x+15$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
          ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
