          ":question/problem": "Which of the following equations has infinitely many solutions?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $2\\left(x-4\\right)=2x-8$\n\n- **II.** $5\\left(x+3\\right)=2x+18$\n\n- **III.** $4\\left(x-2\\right)=4x-8$",
          ":question/worked-solution": "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\left(x-4\\right) & =2x-8 \\\\ 2\\cdot x-2\\cdot 4 & =2x-8 \\\\ 2x-8 & =2x-8 \\\\ 2x-8-2x & =2x-8-2x \\\\ -8 & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $2\\left(x-4\\right)=2x-8$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\left(x+3\\right) & =2x+18 \\\\ 5\\cdot x+5\\cdot 3 & =2x+18 \\\\ 5x+15 & =2x+18 \\\\ 5x+15-2x & =2x+18-2x \\\\ 3x+15 & =18 \\\\ 3x+15-15 & =18-15 \\\\ 3x & =3 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $5\\left(x+3\\right)=2x+18$ has the unique solution $x=1.$\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\left(x-2\\right) & =4x-8 \\\\ 4\\cdot x-4\\cdot 2 & =4x-8 \\\\ 4x-8 & =4x-8 \\\\ 4x-8-4x & =4x-8-4x \\\\ -8 & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $x.$ Therefore, the equation $4\\left(x-2\\right)=4x-8$ has infinitely many solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
